  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Breaking change: query filters are now request objects, and CancellationToken is mandatory.\n\n      The major version stays at 9 because it tracks the version of the HubSpot API this library\n      targets, not the shape of the library's own surface. Read the version number as \"HubSpot API\n      v9\"; it does not follow semantic versioning, so a major version that has not moved is not a\n      promise that your code still compiles. This release will not compile against existing calls.\n\n      Endpoints that took a list of optional query parameters now take one request object:\n\n        Before: GetPageAsync(limit: 10, properties: [\"name\"], cancellationToken: ct)\n        After:  GetPageAsync(new CrmPageRequest { Limit = 10, Properties = [\"name\"] }, ct)\n\n        Before: GetAsync(id, properties: [\"name\"], cancellationToken: ct)\n        After:  GetAsync(id, new CrmGetRequest { Properties = [\"name\"] }, ct)\n\n      CrmPageRequest derives from CrmGetRequest, adding Limit and After, so a caller that already\n      has filters for one endpoint can reuse them across object types. IDomains.GetPageAsync takes\n      DomainPageRequest. Where no filters are wanted, pass new().\n\n      Every CancellationToken parameter has lost its \"= default\" and is now required. Calls that\n      relied on the default will no longer compile; pass the token explicitly. Calls that passed it\n      by name (cancellationToken: ct) still compile once the new request argument is supplied.\n\n      The query string sent to HubSpot is unchanged: each request property carries an explicit\n      AliasAs, and the outgoing URI is now covered by tests that need no portal credentials.\n\n      Motivation: the optional parameters drew 92 static analysis findings, and the six-parameter\n      filter block repeated across nine interfaces was the bulk of the library's reported code\n      duplication. Both are resolved by the single shared request type.",
